# ---> Other editors and tools
# Vim
*.swp
*.swo

# ctags / etags. Anchored: unanchored `tags` would also match a `tags/` module.
/tags
/TAGS

# direnv's cache. .envrc itself stays tracked.
.direnv/

# Patch and merge-conflict artefacts
*.orig
*.rej

# ---> AI coding agents
# The tracked/ignored split matters here: instruction files are project
# documentation and belong in the repo, while per-machine settings, session
# state and chat history do not. Only the latter are listed.

# Claude Code. CLAUDE.md stays tracked as project documentation; the whole
# .claude/ directory is treated as machine-local, so settings, commands,
# session ids and scratch state are all ignored together.
CLAUDE.local.md
.claude/

# Codex and other AGENTS.md-based agents. AGENTS.md itself stays tracked.
AGENTS.local.md
.codex/

# aider chat history and repo map cache
.aider*

# Continue, Gemini CLI, and generic agent scratch directories
.continue/
.gemini/
.agent/

# ChatGPT has no standard project-level artefact; anything it writes is
# ad hoc, so add project-specific patterns here as they appear.

# ---> Environment
# Local secrets and machine-specific configuration. Templates stay tracked so
# the required variables remain documented; the filled-in files never do.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.template

# ---> Deliberately omitted
# The following appear in the upstream github/gitignore templates but are too
# broad for a file shared across every language. Add them to an individual
# project's .gitignore where that project actually needs them.
#
#   [Bb]in/ [Oo]bj/ [Dd]ebug/ [Rr]elease/ x64/ x86/
#       Visual Studio build directories. `bin/` in particular is commonly a
#       tracked directory of project scripts.
#   out/            JetBrains output directory; also a common tracked name.
#   /lib/ /classes/ Clojure. `lib/` is tracked in most C and C++ projects.
#   /doc/           Elixir generated docs; `doc/` is often handwritten.
#   *.d             C dependency files, but also every D language source file.
#   *.mod           Linux kernel module lists, but also go.mod, which every Go
#                   project tracks. No narrower form exists, because the
#                   kernel's own .mod files are plainly named.
#   *.cmd           Linux kernel build command files, but also every Windows
#                   batch script anywhere in the tree. The kernel's are
#                   dot-prefixed, so `.*.cmd` in the kernel section above keeps
#                   them without the collision.
#   *.out           Covered more precisely by `a.out` above.
#   *.zip *.tar.gz  luarocks artefacts; catches legitimate test fixtures.
#   *.log           Catches intentionally committed log fixtures.
#   bin/ tmp/       Eclipse scratch directories; both are commonly tracked.
#   public          Gatsby build output, but the standard name for tracked
#                   static assets in nearly every other web framework.
#   logs coverage   Node, in bare unanchored form; both match tracked
#                   directories elsewhere. Anchored /coverage/ is used instead.
